1
00:00:00,580 --> 00:00:02,820
Meera's friend is a chartered accountant.

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:05,620
She stops by for chai, spots Meera's khata,

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:07,540
and starts firing rules.

4
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:08,660
Debit the receiver!

5
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:09,700
Credit the giver!

6
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:12,260
Debit what comes in, credit what goes out!

7
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:15,140
Debit all expenses, credit all incomes!

8
00:00:15,862 --> 00:00:17,702
Meera's head is spinning.

9
00:00:17,942 --> 00:00:19,622
Is this a whole new system?

10
00:00:20,713 --> 00:00:21,753
It isn't.

11
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:24,873
It's the same language, in another dialect.

12
00:00:25,073 --> 00:00:26,633
Today, we translate it.

13
00:00:32,486 --> 00:00:34,406
First, last time's questions.

14
00:00:34,966 --> 00:00:35,566
One.

15
00:00:35,846 --> 00:00:38,726
Meera buys six thousand rupees of tea, milk

16
00:00:38,806 --> 00:00:40,886
and sugar, and pays cash.

17
00:00:41,006 --> 00:00:43,206
What's debited, and what's credited?

18
00:00:44,370 --> 00:00:46,690
Debit Stock, six thousand.

19
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:49,250
An asset grows, on its home side.

20
00:00:49,490 --> 00:00:51,730
Credit Cash, six thousand.

21
00:00:51,810 --> 00:00:53,330
An asset shrinks.

22
00:00:53,878 --> 00:00:54,558
Two.

23
00:00:54,838 --> 00:00:56,998
Why is an expense a debit?

24
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:00,560
Because an expense shrinks equity.

25
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:03,200
Move it across the equals sign, and it sits

26
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:05,200
on the left, with the assets.

27
00:01:05,761 --> 00:01:06,161
Three.

28
00:01:06,401 --> 00:01:09,201
Imagine Meera pays a bill from the bank.

29
00:01:09,441 --> 00:01:11,761
The SMS says debited.

30
00:01:11,841 --> 00:01:13,041
In Meera's books?

31
00:01:14,197 --> 00:01:15,077
Credited.

32
00:01:15,317 --> 00:01:18,597
Bank is an asset, and it shrank, so it goes

33
00:01:18,677 --> 00:01:19,477
on the right.

34
00:01:20,747 --> 00:01:23,227
Now, Indian traders have kept double-entry

35
00:01:23,307 --> 00:01:26,347
books, the bahi-khata, for centuries.

36
00:01:26,427 --> 00:01:27,867
Naam, for debit.

37
00:01:27,907 --> 00:01:29,467
Jama, for credit.

38
00:01:29,547 --> 00:01:31,387
And khata means account.

39
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:33,707
That's where our Khata gets its name.

40
00:01:34,856 --> 00:01:37,176
The golden rules start by sorting every

41
00:01:37,416 --> 00:01:39,736
account into one of three families.

42
00:01:39,936 --> 00:01:42,776
Personal accounts are people and organisations:

43
00:01:42,856 --> 00:01:45,976
Ravi Mama, Gopal Dairy, Infotech.

44
00:01:46,056 --> 00:01:49,336
Real accounts are things: cash, the cart,

45
00:01:49,376 --> 00:01:50,296
the stock.

46
00:01:50,416 --> 00:01:52,456
And Nominal accounts are money made and

47
00:01:52,536 --> 00:01:55,816
money spent: sales, rent, salary.

48
00:01:56,380 --> 00:01:56,900
Quick.

49
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:58,300
Ravi Mama's loan?

50
00:01:59,861 --> 00:02:00,501
Personal.

51
00:02:00,901 --> 00:02:01,861
He's a person.

52
00:02:02,341 --> 00:02:03,221
The kettle?

53
00:02:04,779 --> 00:02:05,339
Real.

54
00:02:05,659 --> 00:02:06,779
You can hold it.

55
00:02:07,838 --> 00:02:09,598
Each family has one rule.

56
00:02:10,078 --> 00:02:13,118
Real: debit what comes in, credit what goes

57
00:02:13,198 --> 00:02:13,518
out.

58
00:02:14,518 --> 00:02:15,558
The translation?

59
00:02:15,798 --> 00:02:17,478
Real accounts are assets.

60
00:02:17,878 --> 00:02:20,438
Something coming in grows an asset, and

61
00:02:20,518 --> 00:02:22,278
assets grow on the left.

62
00:02:22,358 --> 00:02:23,158
Same thing.

63
00:02:23,919 --> 00:02:26,639
Personal: debit the receiver, credit the

64
00:02:26,679 --> 00:02:27,439
giver.

65
00:02:28,119 --> 00:02:30,039
When someone gives the stall something,

66
00:02:30,119 --> 00:02:32,919
like a loan, the stall now owes them.

67
00:02:32,999 --> 00:02:35,319
A liability grows, on the right.

68
00:02:35,399 --> 00:02:36,359
A credit.

69
00:02:36,939 --> 00:02:39,099
And when someone receives from the stall,

70
00:02:39,179 --> 00:02:42,139
they owe us, or we owe them less.

71
00:02:42,219 --> 00:02:43,179
That's the left.

72
00:02:43,259 --> 00:02:43,979
A debit.

73
00:02:44,731 --> 00:02:48,171
Nominal: debit all expenses, credit all

74
00:02:48,251 --> 00:02:48,811
incomes.

75
00:02:49,811 --> 00:02:52,651
Expenses shrink equity, so they're debits.

76
00:02:52,851 --> 00:02:55,171
Incomes grow it, so they're credits.

77
00:02:56,427 --> 00:02:57,427
Let's prove it.

78
00:02:57,627 --> 00:02:58,667
Two cameras again.

79
00:02:58,907 --> 00:03:01,227
Golden rules on this side, the scale on

80
00:03:01,347 --> 00:03:02,027
that side.

81
00:03:02,591 --> 00:03:05,351
The cart, thirty-six thousand rupees.

82
00:03:05,631 --> 00:03:07,391
Which two things changed?

83
00:03:09,346 --> 00:03:13,226
Golden rules: the cart comes in, debit Equipment.

84
00:03:13,426 --> 00:03:15,746
Cash goes out, credit Cash.

85
00:03:16,486 --> 00:03:20,086
The scale: one asset grows, one shrinks.

86
00:03:20,833 --> 00:03:23,393
Gopal's stock on credit, eight thousand.

87
00:03:23,793 --> 00:03:25,673
Stock comes in: debit.

88
00:03:25,873 --> 00:03:27,913
Gopal is the giver: credit.

89
00:03:28,113 --> 00:03:31,393
The scale: an asset up, a liability up.

90
00:03:32,161 --> 00:03:34,161
Cash sales, eighteen thousand.

91
00:03:34,561 --> 00:03:36,401
Cash comes in: debit.

92
00:03:36,601 --> 00:03:38,721
Sales is an income: credit.

93
00:03:38,761 --> 00:03:41,921
The scale: an asset up, equity up.

94
00:03:42,001 --> 00:03:44,721
Every time, both methods write exactly the

95
00:03:44,841 --> 00:03:45,681
same entry.

96
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:49,180
Two traps catch almost everyone.

97
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:51,140
First, the bank.

98
00:03:51,420 --> 00:03:54,060
Meera's money sits in it, so the bank feels

99
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:54,780
like a thing.

100
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:56,540
A Real account, right?

101
00:03:57,261 --> 00:03:57,781
Nope.

102
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:01,501
The bank is an organisation, so it's Personal.

103
00:04:02,081 --> 00:04:04,641
When Meera deposited fifteen thousand rupees,

104
00:04:04,721 --> 00:04:06,481
the bank was the receiver.

105
00:04:06,561 --> 00:04:07,761
Debit Bank.

106
00:04:08,041 --> 00:04:10,081
Exactly what we found with the SMS.

107
00:04:10,835 --> 00:04:12,595
Second trap: capital.

108
00:04:13,075 --> 00:04:16,115
It's Meera's money, so is it Real?

109
00:04:16,275 --> 00:04:16,835
No.

110
00:04:17,075 --> 00:04:19,795
Capital is Meera's own account, and Meera

111
00:04:19,955 --> 00:04:20,835
is a person.

112
00:04:21,075 --> 00:04:22,995
On day one, she was the giver.

113
00:04:23,235 --> 00:04:25,395
Credit Capital, fifty thousand.

114
00:04:26,659 --> 00:04:28,699
So here's your translation card.

115
00:04:28,979 --> 00:04:30,899
Real means assets.

116
00:04:31,139 --> 00:04:34,419
Personal means people: anyone who owes us,

117
00:04:34,539 --> 00:04:37,459
anyone we owe, the bank, the owner's capital

118
00:04:37,539 --> 00:04:38,499
and drawings.

119
00:04:39,239 --> 00:04:42,199
And advances: a customer the stall owes

120
00:04:42,399 --> 00:04:44,199
something to counts as Personal too.

121
00:04:44,759 --> 00:04:48,039
Nominal means incomes and expenses: everything

122
00:04:48,119 --> 00:04:49,959
that ends up in the Profit and Loss.

123
00:04:51,212 --> 00:04:52,212
Let's practise.

124
00:04:52,412 --> 00:04:53,852
Khata goes first.

125
00:04:53,932 --> 00:04:56,652
Ravi Mama lends thirty thousand rupees.

126
00:04:57,219 --> 00:04:58,099
Cash comes in.

127
00:04:58,339 --> 00:05:01,939
Real, so debit Cash, thirty thousand.

128
00:05:02,519 --> 00:05:03,999
Ravi Mama is the giver.

129
00:05:04,199 --> 00:05:07,799
Personal, so credit his loan, thirty thousand.

130
00:05:07,879 --> 00:05:08,719
And the scale?

131
00:05:08,919 --> 00:05:10,839
An asset up, a liability up.

132
00:05:11,239 --> 00:05:11,959
Match.

133
00:05:12,730 --> 00:05:13,530
Meera's turn.

134
00:05:14,010 --> 00:05:15,930
Rent, five thousand rupees.

135
00:05:16,330 --> 00:05:17,610
Rent is an expense.

136
00:05:18,010 --> 00:05:20,090
Nominal, so debit Rent.

137
00:05:20,490 --> 00:05:21,370
Now the other line.

138
00:05:21,770 --> 00:05:23,050
Cash leaves the galla.

139
00:05:23,530 --> 00:05:24,370
Which family?

140
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:25,450
Which rule?

141
00:05:27,799 --> 00:05:29,479
Real, and it goes out.

142
00:05:29,959 --> 00:05:32,119
Credit Cash, five thousand.

143
00:05:32,963 --> 00:05:34,243
Now, just you.

144
00:05:34,483 --> 00:05:35,723
April twenty-fifth.

145
00:05:35,923 --> 00:05:38,163
Infotech pays its four thousand rupee bill

146
00:05:38,243 --> 00:05:39,443
by UPI.

147
00:05:39,523 --> 00:05:42,083
Debit Bank, credit Infotech, that part you

148
00:05:42,163 --> 00:05:42,643
know.

149
00:05:42,723 --> 00:05:44,243
Now tag both lines.

150
00:05:47,394 --> 00:05:48,834
The bank receives the money.

151
00:05:49,314 --> 00:05:52,634
Personal, the receiver, so debit Bank.

152
00:05:52,834 --> 00:05:54,274
Infotech gives it.

153
00:05:54,514 --> 00:05:57,514
Personal, the giver, so credit Infotech.

154
00:05:57,714 --> 00:05:58,674
Both Personal!

155
00:05:59,674 --> 00:06:02,554
And the scale agrees: one asset grows, one

156
00:06:02,634 --> 00:06:03,434
shrinks.

157
00:06:03,474 --> 00:06:04,874
A swap on the left.

158
00:06:06,143 --> 00:06:06,543
So.

159
00:06:06,623 --> 00:06:10,743
Three families: Personal, Real, Nominal.

160
00:06:11,023 --> 00:06:13,583
Three rules, and each one is the scale in

161
00:06:13,663 --> 00:06:14,543
other words.

162
00:06:14,623 --> 00:06:17,023
Two dialects, the same entries.

163
00:06:18,244 --> 00:06:18,844
Your turn.

164
00:06:19,044 --> 00:06:20,084
Three quick ones.

165
00:06:20,324 --> 00:06:20,804
One.

166
00:06:21,044 --> 00:06:23,244
The four thousand rupee catering advance.

167
00:06:23,444 --> 00:06:25,844
Which golden rule goes on each line?

168
00:06:26,164 --> 00:06:26,644
Two.

169
00:06:26,884 --> 00:06:29,284
Is the bank Real, or Personal?

170
00:06:29,604 --> 00:06:30,124
Three.

171
00:06:30,404 --> 00:06:32,484
Which family does rent belong to?

172
00:06:32,724 --> 00:06:34,404
Answers next lesson.

173
00:06:35,864 --> 00:06:37,898
And now, Checkpoint three.

174
00:06:38,138 --> 00:06:40,538
Here are six of Aman's transactions.

175
00:06:40,618 --> 00:06:43,258
For each one, write the debit, the credit,

176
00:06:43,378 --> 00:06:45,818
and the golden rule behind each line.

177
00:06:45,938 --> 00:06:47,738
Pause the video, and try them now.

178
00:06:50,779 --> 00:06:51,099
Ready?

179
00:06:51,659 --> 00:06:51,979
One.

180
00:06:52,219 --> 00:06:54,219
The ten-thousand-rupee bank loan.

181
00:06:54,619 --> 00:06:57,339
Debit Cash, Real, it comes in.

182
00:06:57,539 --> 00:07:00,379
Credit Bank loan, Personal, the giver.

183
00:07:00,699 --> 00:07:01,139
Two.

184
00:07:01,339 --> 00:07:03,819
The cart and fryer, eighteen thousand.

185
00:07:03,899 --> 00:07:05,899
Debit Cart, credit Cash.

186
00:07:06,059 --> 00:07:08,059
Real in, Real out.

187
00:07:08,219 --> 00:07:08,779
Three.

188
00:07:08,859 --> 00:07:10,579
Nine thousand of cash sales.

189
00:07:10,779 --> 00:07:13,979
Debit Cash, credit Sales, a Nominal income.

190
00:07:14,628 --> 00:07:15,428
Four.

191
00:07:15,508 --> 00:07:17,428
Two thousand of stall rent.

192
00:07:17,508 --> 00:07:20,028
Debit Stall rent, a Nominal expense.

193
00:07:20,228 --> 00:07:21,548
Credit Cash.

194
00:07:21,828 --> 00:07:22,548
Five.

195
00:07:22,628 --> 00:07:24,468
Fifteen hundred of samosas to the school

196
00:07:24,548 --> 00:07:26,308
canteen, on credit.

197
00:07:26,388 --> 00:07:29,108
Debit the canteen, Personal, the receiver.

198
00:07:29,268 --> 00:07:30,748
Credit Sales.

199
00:07:31,028 --> 00:07:31,748
Six.

200
00:07:31,828 --> 00:07:33,788
The thousand-rupee birthday advance.

201
00:07:33,988 --> 00:07:34,948
Debit Cash.

202
00:07:35,108 --> 00:07:37,668
Credit Advance from customer, Personal,

203
00:07:37,708 --> 00:07:38,348
the giver.

204
00:07:38,628 --> 00:07:39,908
How many did you get?

205
00:07:41,402 --> 00:07:42,362
Next time.

206
00:07:42,602 --> 00:07:43,882
April thirtieth.

207
00:07:43,962 --> 00:07:46,282
Five things happen before lunch, and Meera

208
00:07:46,362 --> 00:07:48,682
grabs a tissue to write them all down.
